<% content_for :title, "Bipolar & Addiction | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="addiction-escaping-page bipolar-addiction-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-hero">
    <div class="addiction-escaping-hero-content">

      <p class="addiction-escaping-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / ADDICTION / BIPOLAR &amp; ADDICTION
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>BIPOLAR</span>
        <span>&amp;</span>
        <span>ADDICTION.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="addiction-escaping-intro">
        THE MOODS. THE URGES. THE CONSEQUENCES.<br>
        UNDERSTANDING HOW THEY GET TANGLED TOGETHER.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / THE RELATIONSHIP -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-pink">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">01 / THE RELATIONSHIP</p>

      <h2>
        ANOTHER<br>
        NICE<br>
        MESS.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <blockquote>
        “Well, here’s another nice mess you’ve gotten me into.”
        <br><br>
        — Oliver Hardy
      </blockquote>

      <p class="ae-lead">
        You want relief from one experience and end up with
        consequences that complicate the other.
      </p>

      <p>
        Bipolar disorder and addiction can become tangled together
        until it is hard to tell what you are managing, what you
        are escaping, and what is making everything worse.
      </p>

      <p>
        You take your medication. You care about recovery.
        You can still struggle with impulsivity, cravings,
        and episodes. Difficulty does not mean you have
        stopped trying.
      </p>

      <p>
        Both conditions deserve attention, including the patterns
        that connect them and the ones that continue when
        your mood is stable.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / MANIA -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-yellow">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">02 / MANIA &amp; IMPULSIVITY</p>

      <h2>
        WANT IT.<br>
        DO IT.<br>
        FUCK IT.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p class="ae-lead">
        The space between an idea and an action can get
        dangerously small.
      </p>

      <p>
        Spending, drinking, or getting high can become part
        of the same urgent pursuit of an experience. You want
        something, and the reasons to wait barely register.
      </p>

      <p>
        You might recognize the consequences while feeling
        unusually confident that you can handle them.
        Another person’s concern can seem unnecessary
        when your own judgment feels completely convincing.
      </p>

      <div class="ae-callout">
        CERTAINTY DESERVES<br>
        A SECOND LOOK.
      </div>

      <p>
        Prepare safeguards while you have room to consider them.
        Let trusted people and your treatment team have a place
        in the plan before your judgment becomes harder
        to rely on.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / DEPRESSION AND MIXED SYMPTOMS -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-purple">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">03 / WANTING RELIEF</p>

      <h2>
        SOMETHING<br>
        NEEDS TO<br>
        CHANGE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p>
        Your depressive episodes can last four months or longer.
        You get tired of the heaviness, the waiting, and finding
        ways to get through another day.
      </p>

      <p>
        Alcohol or weed may promise an interruption:
        something to anticipate, a different feeling,
        an evening that does not resemble the one before it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Mixed symptoms bring another kind of pressure.
        You feel miserable, but your thoughts keep moving.
        You want to escape and act at the same time.
        Something needs to change right fucking now.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        WHAT WILL THIS CHANGE NOW?<br>
        WHAT WILL IT LEAVE YOU WITH TOMORROW?
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        The need for relief deserves care. Bring support closer
        when urgency starts making the consequences
        feel irrelevant.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / BETWEEN EPISODES -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-black">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">04 / BETWEEN EPISODES</p>

      <h2>
        THE MOOD<br>
        SETTLES.<br>
        THE URGE<br>
        REMAINS.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p class="ae-lead">
        Your addiction deserves attention when your mood
        is stable, too.
      </p>

      <p>
        Cravings, familiar routines, boredom, and selective
        memories of drinking can still show up.
        Stability does not automatically resolve your
        relationship with substances.
      </p>

      <p>
        Ask what is happening in this particular moment.
        Sometimes your mood is central. Sometimes an old
        association has been stirred up. Sometimes several
        things are happening together.
      </p>

      <p>
        You do not need one explanation for every urge.
        Understanding the pattern starts with noticing
        the experience you are actually having.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / AWARENESS -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-pink">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">05 / NOTICE WHAT IS HAPPENING</p>

      <h2>
        FEEL IT.<br>
        QUESTION<br>
        THE STORY.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p>
        Begin by acknowledging your suffering. Your mood
        is difficult. Your body hurts. You are restless,
        ashamed, or overwhelmed. These experiences
        deserve attention.
      </p>

      <p>
        Then notice what you add: you should be able to
        handle this, you are weak for wanting relief,
        you have ruined everything already.
        Those judgments give you more pain to escape.
      </p>

      <p>
        Observe the urge. What does it promise?
        What sensations accompany it? What thoughts
        make using sound reasonable?
      </p>

      <p>
        “You deserve a break” may identify a real need.
        “Therefore, you should drink” adds a conclusion
        worth questioning. “You already fucked up,
        so it does not matter” overlooks the choices
        still available.
      </p>

      <div class="ae-callout">
        AN URGE CAN BE PRESENT<br>
        WHILE YOU CONSIDER YOUR NEXT STEP.
      </div>

      <p>
        When a pause is available, use it.
        When your ability to pause is slipping,
        support and practical safeguards need
        to do more of the work.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / ACCEPTANCE AND INTERNAL CONFLICT -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-yellow">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">06 / TIRED OF MANAGING EVERYTHING</p>

      <h2>
        PART OF YOU<br>
        WANTS OUT.<br>
        LISTEN.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p>
        You have bipolar disorder. Alcohol and cannabis
        have become problems in your life.
        Managing both can be fucking exhausting.
        You can acknowledge that without giving up
        on what might help.
      </p>

      <p>
        A part of you may want freedom, excitement,
        or a few hours without being responsible
        for your own mind. What has become unbearable?
        What is that part hoping using will provide?
      </p>

      <p>
        Other parts want stability, connection,
        and a future with more choices.
        Give those needs room in the decision, too.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        YOU CAN UNDERSTAND THE DESIRE TO ESCAPE<br>
        AND STILL PROTECT YOURSELF.
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Your changing mental states do not account
        for your entire identity. Your values,
        relationships, abilities, and dignity
        remain part of who you are.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 07 / PRACTICAL RESPONSE -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-purple">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">07 / THE NEXT AVAILABLE ACTION</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT CAN<br>
        YOU DO<br>
        RIGHT NOW?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p class="ae-lead">
        Some days, the task is getting through a difficult
        stretch without escalating it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Stop, step back, and describe what is happening.
        Close the shopping app. Leave the place where
        using is becoming easier. Tell someone you are
        starting to bargain.
      </p>

      <p>
        Treat the situation as a challenge in resourcefulness.
        Given what you are experiencing, what response
        would protect something you care about?
      </p>

      <p>
        You cannot undo previous episodes or guarantee
        that another will never occur. You can prepare,
        involve support, and reconsider a plan
        that is no longer sufficient.
      </p>

      <p>
        Spiritual reflection and coping skills belong
        alongside clinical treatment. If impulsivity
        escalates, psychosis appears, or you feel unable
        to stay safe, seek prompt professional help.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 08 / ACCOUNTABILITY -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-black">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">08 / ACCOUNTABILITY &amp; COMPASSION</p>

      <h2>
        FACE IT.<br>
        REPAIR IT.<br>
        KEEP GOING.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p>
        Your conditions can help explain what happened,
        while the consequences still need attention.
        Address what you can repair. Listen when someone
        describes harm. Reconsider safeguards
        that were insufficient.
      </p>

      <p>
        Keep the focus specific enough to guide change.
        Repeatedly declaring yourself a terrible person
        gives you very little to work with.
      </p>

      <div class="ae-callout">
        YOU HAVE RESPONSIBILITIES.<br>
        YOU ARE WORTHY OF CARE.
      </div>

      <p>
        Let go of the demand that doing everything
        responsibly must produce perfect stability.
        Your effort still matters when progress
        is slow or uncertain.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-ending">

    <div>

      <p class="ae-ending-small">
        GIVE YOUR LIFE ROOM TO GROW.
      </p>

      <h2>
        MORE CHOICE.<br>
        MORE CONNECTION.<br>
        <span>MORE LIFE.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="ae-ending-copy">

      <p>
        Your creative work, relationships, interests,
        and ordinary routines deserve attention.
        Stability and sobriety can protect your
        ability to participate in those things.
      </p>

      <p>
        You do not need to solve your entire relationship
        with bipolar disorder and addiction at once.
        Keep noticing what is happening, asking what
        you need, and using the resources available.
      </p>

      <p>
        Return to your values when you lose sight of them.
        Let support matter. Keep building a life
        you want to remain present for.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO ADDICTION",
          addiction_path,
          class: "ae-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>